            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest notable news is that NASA’s Perseverance rover captured a first-of-its-kind view from Mars: Earth disappearing behind the Martian moon Phobos. NASA/JPL published the story on Aug. 5, 2026, based on images taken by Perseverance’s Mastcam-Z camera on July 2, 2026 — sol 1,907 of the mission. In the sequence, Earth appears as a tiny bright dot and is briefly occulted by the crescent of Phobos, making it the first time Earth has been seen “vanishing” behind another object from the surface of another planet【5:0†source】.\n\nOther recent Perseverance updates include NASA’s July 2026 reports from the rover’s work near “Broom Point” on the rim of Jezero Crater. Perseverance has been studying layered bedrock there, with NASA reporting evidence that a 245-foot-thick — about 75-meter — stack of ancient rock was built by repeated asteroid impacts more than 3.9 billion years ago【5:3†source】. NASA’s Mars image feed also shows Perseverance is still actively returning new imagery, including an “Image of the Week” for July 26–Aug. 1, 2026【5:2†source】.",
                "annotations": [
                  {
                    "type": "url_citation",
                    "url": "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasas-perseverance-rover-watches-earth-vanish-behind-martian-moon/",
